#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158543 :tutorial/content "Given the graph of a curve $y= f(x),$ there are two types of reflections we can apply to it:\n\n1\\. Reflection across the $y$-axis. This is a transformation of the form \n\n  $$\n  y=f(-x).\n  $$\n\n2\\. Reflection across the $x$-axis. This is a transformation of the form \n\n  $$\n  y=-f(x).\n  $$\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ below. \n\n![](images/69/6931216dd63985631394e3f3f2fe59246ddd810ecce5af0f0c7ad18b8634133b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(-x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the $y$-axis, and it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e2/e2274a68c25ed14ba93410fe87d672f85b839b7214b30e80d34a3ae28f47ef03.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=-f(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the $x$-axis, and it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ee/eee560bdd17899d55cdaea35204e9963ae9f0148f0ef897173e6250a3eb874b3.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can also combine reflections with other graph transformations. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "f4bb9366-7e90-51e5-944c-9609c7607cfa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5125 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
